@@ -139,6 +139,7 @@ All values are overridable in `pipeline.user.yaml` — the "Pro" preset, for ins
 - **Must:** naming a model correctly in the dispatch briefing's TEXT (the mandatory "Dispatch-Metadaten"/"dispatch metadata" field, `docs/operating-model.md` §2.1) is necessary but **not sufficient** to route a dispatch to that model. When the target subagent definition (e.g. `plugins/pipeline-core/agents/critic.md`) pins its own `model:` in frontmatter, that pin wins over whatever the dispatch text names — the frontmatter value is what the runner actually reads. **Only the `model` parameter passed on the actual dispatch tool call itself (the Agent/Task invocation, not the prompt text inside it) is authoritative for changing which model runs.** Therefore:
   1. A real MP-05/MP-07-class escalation (Goldfish → higher tier, or Critic → Design-tier model on criticality) MUST set the tool-layer `model` parameter on the dispatch call — stating the escalated model in the briefing text alone does not move the dispatch off the subagent definition's pinned default.
   2. Before dispatching an intended escalation, the dispatcher SHOULD check the target subagent's own frontmatter `model:` value so a text-named escalation doesn't silently no-op against a pin that would otherwise route back to the default tier.
+  3. **The `effort` parameter sits next to the `model` parameter (C23, draft ADR "a project's `pipeline.user.yaml` owns its model and effort routing").** The Agent tool's per-invocation `effort` overrides the frontmatter `effort:` field of the subagent definition (`CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` beats both; non-fork subagents only; Claude Code v2.1.292 or later), so the generated dispatch call carries the configured effort and a frontmatter effort that differs is a warning, never a refusal. The same tool takes only family aliases for `model`: a configured full Claude model id reaches it as its family alias (opus, sonnet, haiku, fable) and the generated call warns that the tool layer carries only the alias. Where the host ignores the effort parameter the dispatch warns, and the effort that really ran is the `effort.level` the host reports on SubagentStop, or unobserved when it reports none; an effort that was not observed is never claimed.
 - **Why (live incident):** a mandated MP-07 escalation — a Critic dispatch with "opus at max" named explicitly in the dispatch text — actually ran on `sonnet` instead of the intended higher tier. Root cause: `plugins/pipeline-core/agents/critic.md` pins `model: sonnet` in its own frontmatter (see its "MODEL ESCALATION (MP-07)" comment: "one agent, model raised PER DISPATCH — the Elephant passes the model invocation parameter... together with the mandatory briefing"), and that pin is what actually governed which model ran, not the prose in the dispatch text. Neither this policy nor CLAUDE.md's "Model discipline" Hard Rule previously documented this override mechanism, so a correctly-worded escalation could silently fail to take effect with no visible symptom at dispatch time.
 - **How to check:** compare the target subagent's frontmatter `model:` value (`plugins/pipeline-core/agents/*.md`) against the dispatch record/telemetry line's actual model-used field for any dispatch whose briefing text names a non-default model; a mismatch where the frontmatter default ran instead of the text-named escalation is a routing defect, not a policy violation by the dispatcher — the fix is to always pass the tool-layer `model` parameter for an intended escalation, never text alone.
 
